The Singular Value Decomposition has a rich history spanning over 150 years, with contributions from Beltrami, Jordan, Eckart & Young, Lanczos, and many others. Stewart's 1993 overview tells this story well. Today SVD is everywhere: dimensionality reduction, recommender systems, numerical linear algebra, signal processing.

For most practitioners, SVD is a single line: `u, s, v = torch.linalg.svd(A)`. The factorization $A = U\Sigma V^T$ is derived by finding eigenvectors of $A^T A$ and $AA^T$, recognizing they share the same nonzero eigenvalues, and relating the two bases through the singular values. But there is another formulation: derive SVD from a **variational principle**, finding unit vectors $u$ and $v$ that maximize their coupling $u^T A v$. This is not merely an alternative derivation. It is the entry point to generalizations that the factorization view obscures.

In 2016, Johan Suykens showed that this variational characterization fits naturally within the Least Squares Support Vector Machine (LS-SVM) framework. The payoff: SVD becomes kernelizable, with asymmetric kernels, two distinct feature maps, and out-of-sample extensions. More recently, this kernel SVD framework has been connected to the attention mechanism in transformers, offering both theoretical insight and practical efficiency gains.

This post develops that foundation. We start with Gilbert Strang's geometric view of SVD, then move to the classical variational formulation, and finally to Suykens' reformulation. Part 2 will show how this leads to [Primal-Attention](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19798).


## The Geometry of SVD

### Four Subspaces and the Circle-to-Ellipse Picture

Gilbert Strang calls SVD the "climax" of linear algebra, and for good reason. It answers a deceptively simple question: given a matrix $A$ that maps vectors from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}^m$, can we find orthonormal bases for both spaces such that $A$ simply maps the $k$-th input basis vector to a scaled version of the $k$-th output basis vector?

The answer is yes, and the scaling factors are the singular values.

What makes this remarkable is the orthogonality constraint. In general, if you pick orthonormal vectors $v_1, \ldots, v_r$ in the input space, their images $Av_1, \ldots, Av_r$ will not be orthogonal. SVD finds the special directions where orthogonality is preserved through the mapping. Strang puts it simply: "orthogonal in, orthogonal out."

This immediately connects the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix. The right singular vectors $V$ split the input space: the first $r$ span the row space, the rest span the null space. The left singular vectors $U$ split the output space: the first $r$ span the column space, the rest span the left null space. SVD gives orthonormal bases for all four, simultaneously.


